      "hints": [
        "Use the volume of revolution formula $V = \\pi \\int_0^2 y^2\\,dx$.",
        "Simplify $y^2 = x^3 + 1$ and integrate term-by-term.",
        "Evaluate between limits $x=0$ and $x=2$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Set up the formula for the volume of revolution about the $x$-axis from $x = 0$ to $x = 2$:\n$$V = \\pi \\int_0^2 y^2\\,dx = \\pi \\int_0^2 \\left((x^3 + 1)^{1/2}\\right)^2\\,dx = \\pi \\int_0^2 (x^3 + 1)\\,dx$$",
        "Integrate $(x^3 + 1)$ with respect to $x$:\n$$\\int (x^3 + 1)\\,dx = \\left[ \\frac{x^4}{4} + x \\right]_0^2$$",
        "Evaluate the definite integral between the limits $x = 0$ and $x = 2$:\n$$V = \\pi \\left( \\left(\\frac{2^4}{4} + 2\\right) - 0 \\right) = \\pi (4 + 2) = 6\\pi \\approx 18.8$$"
      ],
      "checking": {
